  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``\" and \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". The two reply texts are as follows. Text A: \"Here is a fence:\\n```json\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1}\\n{\\\"b\\\": 2}\\n```\\nAnd here is a later top-level object:\\n{\\\"c\\\": 3}\". Text B: \"Here is a top-level object:\\n{\\\"c\\\": 3}\". For Text A, the rule requires the answer to be the last object inside a code fence, which is {\\\"b\\\": 2}, because the fence holds two top-level objects and the last one is {\\\"b\\\": 2}. For Text B, the rule requires the answer to be the last top-level object in the text, which is {\\\"c\\\": 3}, because there is no fence. The function recover_json_object, however, returns {\\\"c\\\": 3} for both texts: in Text A the fenced pool candidate is the entire fenced content '{\\\"a\\\": 1}\\n{\\\"b\\\": 2}', which does not parse as a single JSON object, so it is passed over, and the top-level pool then picks the last top-level object in the whole text, which is {\\\"c\\\": 3}. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. What would make me wrong: if recover_json_object on Text A actually returns {\\\"b\\\": 2} (or any answer other than {\\\"c\\\": 3}), then the function does tell the pair apart and my conjecture is false; likewise if the rule is interpreted to mean that a fenced block containing multiple objects is not a fence that \"holds one\" because the whole block is not a single object, then the rule would require {\\\"c\\\": 3} for Text A as well, and no difference would be required."
